2. Fundamental Mapping

Let a reasoning situation be:

[
Q_t =
P\_t,
G\_t,
S\_t,
C\_t,
I\_t,
U\_t,
B\_t
]

where:

* P\_t = problem class
* G\_t = goal
* S\_t = current semantic state
* C\_t = context
* I\_t = invariants to preserve
* U\_t = uncertainty
* B\_t = available resource budget

The Metamap learns:

[
\mathcal M:
Q_t
\rightarrow
\Pi^*
]

where \\Pi^\* is a recommended strategy policy.

Represent:

[
\Pi^* =
R^\*,
\\Sigma^\*,
F^\*,
\\rho^\*,
\\pi^\*,
\\tau^\*
]

with:

* R^\* = recommended semantic spaces
* \\Sigma^\* = recommended Seals
* F^\* = operator formation
* \\rho^\* = ratios / weighting
* \\pi^\* = portal route
* \\tau^\* = stopping policy

Thus:

[ \boxed{ \mathcal M\\text\{problem\}, \\text\{goal\}, \\text\{state\}

\text{promising reasoning strategy}
}
]

⸻

3. Experience Record

Every semantic traversal produces an experience record.

Define:

[
E_t =
Q\_t,
\\Pi\_t,
O\_t
]

where Q\_t is the initial situation, \\Pi\_t is the deployed strategy, and O\_t is the observed outcome.

The strategy record contains:

[
\Pi_t =
R,
\\Sigma,
F,
\\rho,
\\pi,
\\tau
]

The outcome record contains:

[
O_t =
\\Delta,
L,
P\_I,
N,
C,
S,
F\_a,
V
]

where:

* \\Delta = useful semantic delta
* L = reconstruction loss
* P\_I = invariant preservation
* N = novelty generated
* C = computational / attention cost
* S = surprise
* F\_a = failures encountered
* V = externally validated utility

This record is the raw material of the Metamap.

⸻

4. Afterglow Record

Not everything useful is contained in the final answer.

Each route therefore emits an afterglow:

[
A_t =
\\text\{route\},
\\text\{operator order\},
\\text\{ratios\},
\\text\{invariants\},
\\text\{loss\},
\\Delta,
\\text\{surprise\},
\\text\{failures\},
\\text\{confidence\},
\\text\{cost\}
]

Afterglow is explicitly retained even when the corresponding result is discarded.

The Metamap does not merely learn from success.

It learns from:

* successful reasoning,
* failed reasoning,
* near misses,
* surprising transformations,
* high-loss portals,
* useful distortions,
* redundant routes,
* and emergent operator combinations.

⸻

5. Meta-Features

The Metamap derives features from each reasoning trajectory.

Let:

[
\phiE\_t
]

be the meta-feature extractor.

Possible features include:

[
\phiE\_t=
{
\text{problem topology},
\text{ambiguity},
\text{causal density},
\text{symbolic density},
\text{temporal depth},
\text{uncertainty},
\text{representation mismatch},
\text{operator count},
\text{route depth},
\text{symmetry},
\text{constraint density}
}
]

These features describe the terrain, not merely the content.

The goal is to recognize:

This new problem behaves like previous problems whose successful strategy looked like this.

