13. Explore vs Exploit

The Metamap should not always choose the best-known route.

[
\Pi_t=
\begin{cases}
\text{known high-value strategy}, & \text{exploit}\
\text{novel plausible strategy}, & \text{explore}
\end{cases}
]

Exploration increases when:

* uncertainty is high
* known routes degrade
* terrain is novel
* new Seals/portals appear

⸻

14. Metamap Recursion

Let:

[
M^{0} = \text{object-level experience}
]

[
M^{1} = \text{patterns across experiences}
]

[
M^{2} = \text{patterns across patterns}
]

and:

[
M^{\(k\+1\)} = H\(M^\{\(k\)})
]

Recursion continues only if:

[
\frac{\text{new reusable information}}{\text{added complexity}} > \tau_M
]

Otherwise it is pruned.

⸻

15. Metamap Compression

A mature Metamap compresses experience into strategy fossils:

[
E_1,\ldots,E_{1000}
\rightarrow
F_1,\ldots,F_k
]

Compression quality:

[
Q_C=
\frac{\text{retained predictive utility}}{\text{stored complexity}}
]

Goal: maximize reusable strategy density.

